item+ struct src/phext.rs :: Chain :: pub struct Chain { :: A chain: an ordered series of complete phexts within one stream, joined by CHAIN_BREAK (0x02) and optionally terminated by CHAIN_CLOSE (0x03). The chain layer is a stream-level layer, one rung above the library break: the nine dimension breaks structure a single phext, while the two chain bytes structure a stream of phexts. Each member is a whole phext, worked with the ordinary phext functions in this module; read in succession, a chain's members are editions in sequence — the chain is how a phext stream encodes time. `closed` records how the chain ended: true when a CHAIN_CLOSE terminated it, false when the stream simply ran out (EOF). A chain that ends at EOF is unclosed — possibly truncated — and is never silently promoted to closed. A stream with no chain bytes at all is a single unclosed chain holding one member phext: every pre-0.6 phext reads this way. :: direct :: api
item+ function src/phext.rs :: Chain::fetch :: pub fn fetch(&self, address: &str) -> String { :: Fetch a scroll by multiverse address: "$N:coord", or a bare coordinate (universe 1). Out-of-range universes and missing scrolls yield an empty string, as fetch does. :: direct :: api
item+ function src/phext.rs :: Chain::new :: pub fn new() -> Chain { :: Function Chain::new takes no inputs beyond the value it is called on. It gives back Chain. :: inferred :: api
item+ function src/phext.rs :: Chain::serialize :: pub fn serialize(&self) -> String { :: serializes this chain alone: its members joined by CHAIN_BREAK, with a trailing CHAIN_CLOSE when the chain is closed. See `serialize_chains` for whole streams. :: direct :: api
item+ function src/phext.rs :: parse_chains :: pub fn parse_chains(input: &str) -> Vec<Chain> { :: splits a stream into its chains. CHAIN_BREAK separates the member phexts of a chain; CHAIN_CLOSE terminates a chain, and whatever follows it opens the next one — so one stream may hold many chains. The stream's final chain is closed only when the stream ends with CHAIN_CLOSE. Splitting follows `split_on` semantics, so empty members and empty chains are preserved exactly: parse and serialize are byte-exact inverses for every stream. :: direct :: api
item+ function src/phext.rs :: parse_universe_address :: pub fn parse_universe_address(address: &str) -> Option<(usize, Coordinate)> { :: Function parse_universe_address takes address: &str. It gives back Option<(usize, Coordinate)>. :: inferred :: api
item+ function src/phext.rs :: serialize_chains :: pub fn serialize_chains(chains: &[Chain]) -> String { :: serializes a stream of chains back into stream bytes: the inverse of `parse_chains`. :: direct :: api
