item+ const src/binary.rs :: FIELD_PREFIX :: pub const FIELD_PREFIX: &str = "phext-field:base64:"; :: The prefix of a binary *field*: arbitrary bytes encoded for one line inside a text scroll (a `name=value` grammar, a WAL entry, a header block). Whole scrolls use `encode_scroll`; fields use this. :: direct :: api
item+ function src/binary.rs :: decode_field :: pub fn decode_field(text: &str) -> Option<Vec<u8>> { :: Decodes a field produced by `encode_field`. Returns None when the text lacks the field prefix or its body is not strict base64 — a plain text field is not a binary field, and yields None, not bytes. :: direct :: api
item+ function src/binary.rs :: encode_field :: pub fn encode_field(payload: &[u8]) -> String { :: Encodes raw bytes as a single line of text: the field prefix plus RFC 4648 base64. The result contains no line breaks and none of the nine phext dimension-break bytes, so it can sit inside any line-structured scroll. The base64 overhead (+33%) is inherent to armouring bytes in a text line; this function standardizes the armour so every implementation reads the same field the same way. :: direct :: api
item+ function src/phext.rs :: append :: pub fn append(input: String, location: Coordinate, scroll: &str) -> Option<String> { :: Appends `scroll` at `location`, which must sort strictly after the document's last non-empty scroll. The result is the input with only a delimiter run and the new content added at its end — a host can write just that suffix to a file opened in append mode and sync once. Returns None when the scroll is empty (empty scrolls do not exist) or `location` does not sort after the document's end. Trailing delimiter bytes in the input encode only empty scrolls, so they are dropped first and never shift the appended scroll's address. On None the append was rejected; the input String was consumed, so pass a clone if the caller needs it after a rejected append. :: direct :: api
item+ function src/phext.rs :: append_batch :: pub fn append_batch(input: String, entries: &[(Coordinate, &str)]) -> Option<String> { :: Appends several scrolls in one call. Entries must be non-empty and in strictly ascending coordinate order, the first sorting after the document's end. The whole batch is a single suffix of the result, so a host persists it with one write and one sync. This is construction atomicity, not storage atomicity: whether the suffix lands completely is the host filesystem's business (see the durability profile in the crate docs). Pair the batch with a `commit_scroll` as its final entry to make a torn batch *detectable* at recovery — that is the part a format library can own. :: direct :: api
item+ function src/phext.rs :: append_checked :: pub fn append_checked(input: String, location: Coordinate, scroll: &str) -> Option<String> { :: `append` with an integrity trailer: the stored scroll is `checked_scroll(scroll)`, verifiable later with `fetch_checked`. :: direct :: api
item+ function src/phext.rs :: checked_scroll :: pub fn checked_scroll(scroll: &str) -> String { :: Returns `scroll` with an integrity trailer appended: one final line `phext-check:xxh3-128:<checksum of the payload>`. In a checked scroll the final line is reserved — verification always reads the LAST marker line, and the checksum binds the entire payload before it, so a marker line inside the payload cannot forge a trailer. The checksum detects accidental corruption and torn writes. xxh3 is not a cryptographic hash: this is integrity, not authentication. An empty scroll stays empty (a scroll with no bytes does not exist, so there is nothing to check). :: direct :: api
item+ function src/phext.rs :: commit_scroll :: pub fn commit_scroll(entries: &[(Coordinate, &str)]) -> String { :: Builds the commit marker for a batch: a small scroll binding the batch's entry count and a checksum over each entry's coordinate and content, in order. Append it (via `append_batch`) as the batch's final scroll, at a coordinate after every entry. `entries` here are the (coordinate, content) pairs exactly as appended — checked contents included, when the batch uses checked scrolls. :: direct :: api
item+ function src/phext.rs :: fetch_checked :: pub fn fetch_checked(input: &str, target: Coordinate) -> Option<String> { :: Fetches the scroll at `target` and returns its payload, but only when the scroll is checked and verifies. A missing, unchecked, or torn scroll yields None — recovery code can treat None as "not committed". :: direct :: api
item+ function src/phext.rs :: uncheck_scroll :: pub fn uncheck_scroll(scroll: &str) -> Option<String> { :: Returns the payload of a checked scroll when its trailer verifies; None for unchecked, malformed, or corrupted scrolls. :: direct :: api
item+ function src/phext.rs :: verify_checked_scroll :: pub fn verify_checked_scroll(scroll: &str) -> bool { :: true when `scroll` ends with a well-formed integrity trailer whose checksum matches the payload before it. :: direct :: api
item+ function src/phext.rs :: verify_commit :: pub fn verify_commit(input: &str, entries: &[Coordinate], commit: Coordinate) -> bool { :: true when the scroll at `commit` is a commit marker whose count matches `entries` and whose checksum matches the entries' current contents, fetched in order. Any missing, altered, or torn entry — or a missing marker — yields false: the batch did not commit. :: direct :: api
