item+ struct src/phext.rs :: ScrollSpan :: pub struct ScrollSpan { :: The borrowed form of a `PositionedScroll`: a scroll's coordinate plus the byte range of its text within the input document. Slicing the input with `range` yields exactly the scroll's text — building the index allocates no `String` per scroll. :: direct :: api
item+ function src/phext.rs :: spans :: pub fn spans(input: &str) -> Vec<ScrollSpan> { :: Builds a borrowed span index over `input`: one `ScrollSpan` per scroll, in document order, giving each scroll's coordinate and the byte range of its text in `input`. The walk is the `phokenize` walk — the same dimension breaks and the same coordinate arithmetic — except that it records byte ranges instead of allocating a `String` for every scroll. For every returned span, `&input[span.range]` is exactly the scroll `explode` returns at `span.coord`, and `span.range.start` is the offset `offset` reports for that coordinate. Empty-scroll contract: a scroll exists iff it holds at least one byte — the definition `explode` encodes — so `spans` reports only non-empty scrolls. Delimiter runs that encode empty scrolls (leading breaks, runs between distant scrolls, trailing breaks) produce no span; an empty document, or a document of only delimiters, produces no spans. `phokenize` remains the tokenizer of record for empty slots: it additionally reports a final empty positioned scroll in those trailing cases, where `spans` reports none. Chain bytes (0x02 / 0x03) are not dimension breaks, so — exactly as in `phokenize` and `explode` — they are scroll content here. This index covers a single phext (one universe); chain structure is the `parse_chains` layer above it. Motivating consumer: holo-phext, whose `src/index.rs` carries a 323-line re-implementation of this walk, plus a duplicated private dimension-break predicate in its core, because libphext exported only owned-`String` tokenization (`phokenize`/`explode`) and a single-scroll `offset`. Downstream stores should index with `spans` instead of re-walking the format. # Example ``` use libphext::phext::{spans, to_coordinate}; let input = "alpha\u{17}beta"; let index = spans(input); assert_eq!(index.len(), 2); assert_eq!(index[0].coord, to_coordinate("1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.1")); assert_eq!(&input[index[0].range.clone()], "alpha"); assert_eq!(&input[index[1].range.clone()], "beta"); ``` :: direct :: api
