item+ struct src/phext.rs :: TocChild :: pub struct TocChild { :: One entry in a `toc_children` listing: a value taken by the next dimension below a coordinate prefix, together with the number of scrolls and the total scroll bytes in that child's subtree. Structure only — no scroll text is copied or returned. :: direct :: api
item+ struct src/phext.rs :: TocScroll :: pub struct TocScroll { :: One entry in a `toc_scrolls` listing: a scroll's full coordinate and the byte length of its text. The planet list for progressive clients — coordinates and sizes, never bodies. :: direct :: api
item+ struct src/phext.rs :: TocSummary :: pub struct TocSummary { :: The aggregate shape of a coordinate prefix's subtree: how many scrolls it holds and how many bytes of scroll text they carry in total. :: direct :: api
item+ function src/phext.rs :: toc_children :: pub fn toc_children(input: &str, prefix: &[usize]) -> Vec<TocChild> { :: The progressive table of contents: given a coordinate prefix — the leading `prefix.len()` address components, 0 to 8 of them — returns one `TocChild` per distinct value the next dimension takes under that prefix, sorted ascending, each with the scroll count and total scroll bytes of its subtree. A client walking a phext from the root calls `toc_children(input, &[])` for the libraries present, then `toc_children(input, &[2])` for the shelves of library 2, and so on — downloading structure one level at a time instead of the whole document. Built on the `spans` index in a single pass; scroll bodies are never copied. A prefix of 9 or more components has no next dimension and yields an empty listing, as does a prefix no scroll matches. The children partition their parent: for any prefix shorter than 9 components, the `scrolls` of `toc_children(input, prefix)` sum to `toc_summary(input, prefix).scrolls`, and the `bytes` sum likewise. # Example ``` use libphext::phext::toc_children; let input = "alpha\u{17}beta\u{18}gamma"; let root = toc_children(input, &[]); assert_eq!(root.len(), 1); assert_eq!(root[0].value, 1); assert_eq!(root[0].scrolls, 3); ``` :: direct :: api
item+ function src/phext.rs :: toc_scrolls :: pub fn toc_scrolls(input: &str, prefix: &[usize]) -> Vec<TocScroll> { :: Every scroll under `prefix` (0 to 9 leading address components), in document order, as a `TocScroll`: full coordinate plus byte length, no bodies. This is the planet list a progressive client renders at the bottom of a zoom — it asks for the scrolls of one star system, not of the whole phext. A full 9-component prefix yields the single scroll at that coordinate, if populated. :: direct :: api
item+ function src/phext.rs :: toc_summary :: pub fn toc_summary(input: &str, prefix: &[usize]) -> TocSummary { :: The size of a subtree: the number of scrolls under `prefix` (0 to 9 leading address components) and their total byte length. `toc_summary(input, &[])` summarizes the whole document; a full 9-component prefix summarizes a single scroll — 1 scroll if it is populated, 0 if it is not. :: direct :: api
